OpenBSD security integration
===========================

Linting and list-rules lock unveil to the selected configuration and
input files, then pledge stdio/rpath. No recipes or helper commands run.
A sandbox initialization failure aborts the command. Directory inputs
are rejected rather than exposing entire directory trees. Named FIFO and
other explicit non-directory inputs are retained; /dev/fd and symlink
behavior still require native OpenBSD validation.

Completion generation, completion queries, help and version output remain
outside this parser policy. In particular, Cobra's optional
BASH_COMP_DEBUG_FILE output retains its existing behavior. No claim is
made that those modes provide a filesystem or syscall boundary.

The parser stops on scanner errors, including oversized lines, reports
the read failure and closes the input on all return paths. It does not
silently accept a truncated Makefile. Configuration candidates and input
paths must remain stable during startup; change them and rerun if an
external rename or symlink change invalidates the selected namespace.

OpenBSD compilation, runtime initialization, terminal rendering, config
fallback, invalid-input, descriptor, symlink and denial checks remain
outstanding. No project executable was run to establish these policies.

The kernel limits the number of unveiled names and directories. Very
large input lists may fail during policy setup; split them into smaller
invocations. The port does not expose entire directories to bypass this
limit. A dash argument retains the parser's original meaning of a file
named '-', rather than acquiring a new stdin mode.
